<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">25 April in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 25 April, from 1507 to 1974.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1507 CE <span class="tih-ago">519 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Saint-Dié scholars publish the book that names America</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Cosmographiae Introductio, issued with Martin Waldseemüller's world map, proposed naming the New World 'America' after Amerigo Vespucci. The only surviving copy of the map is in the Library of Congress.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/beijing" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beijing.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1644 CE <span class="tih-ago">382 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Last Ming emperor takes his own life as Beijing falls</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>'s rebels entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor">Chongzhen Emperor</a> hanged himself on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> Hill behind the Forbidden City. Within weeks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> seized the capital and founded Qing rule.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1859 CE <span class="tih-ago">167 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Work begins on the Suez Canal</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-de-lesseps">Ferdinand de Lesseps</a> struck the first blow at the site of Port Said. The canal opened in 1869, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and shortening the sea route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to Asia.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1915 CE <span class="tih-ago">111 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Allied troops land at Gallipoli</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">British, French, Australian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> troops landed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli peninsula</a>. The campaign failed after eight months and around 100,000 deaths on both sides; 25 April is now Anzac Day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1953 CE <span class="tih-ago">73 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Watson and Crick publish the double helix</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Nature published James Watson and Francis Crick's paper on the structure of DNA, alongside papers by Maurice Wilkins and Rosalind Franklin's group. It explained how genetic information is copied.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1974 CE <span class="tih-ago">52 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Carnation Revolution topples Portugal's dictatorship</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A largely bloodless coup by army officers ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> regime. Democracy followed, and Portugal's African colonies gained independence within two years.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a></div></div></li></ol>
